Day 22 - Introduction to Git: My First Repository

What I Learned

Today I started my Git journey and learned the fundamentals of version control.

Topics Covered

 - What is Git and why it is important
- Git configuration (username and email)
- Creating a Git repository
 - Understanding the ".git" directory

- Working Directory, Staging Area, and   Repository
 - Git workflow
- Staging and committing changes
 - Viewing commit history

Commands Practiced

git --version
git config --global user.name
git config --global user.email
git config --list
git init
git status
git add
git commit
git log
git log --oneline
git diff

Key Learnings

- "git add" moves changes to the staging area.
- "git commit" saves changes permanently in Git history.
- "git status" helps track the current state of the repository.
- "git log" shows commit history.

- The ".git" folder stores the entire repository history.

Outcome

- Created my first Git repository.
- Built a Git commands reference file.
- Created multiple commits with meaningful commit messages.
- Learned the complete Git workflow from working directory to repository.